WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.640
Before we continue on to prompt engineering&nbsp;for RAG, let's review the steps that led up to&nbsp;this stage in the process.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.699
Desired knowledge&nbsp;sources are chunked and 
embedded in a vector&nbsp;database,

00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:15.740
where they're indexed and able&nbsp;to be searched 
using semantic similarity.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:21.800
A user query is embedded with the same model then&nbsp;compared to the vectors in the vector database.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.482
This will return several chunks that are the&nbsp;
most similar.

00:00:25.482 --> 00:00:30.507
We now have relevant chunks of&nbsp;the document ready
to be passed to the language&nbsp;model.

00:00:30.507 --> 00:00:37.509
With relevant chunks at hand, we move on to&nbsp;the pivotal step of passing these search results&nbsp;to the language model.

00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:44.057
This instructs the model&nbsp;to use the retrieved information to provide&nbsp;a more informed answer to the user's query.

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:50.933
Generating the most accurate, thorough, relevant&nbsp;output possible belongs to the realm of prompt&nbsp;engineering.

00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:56.124
Prompt engineering is the process&nbsp;or art of 
designing text instructions

00:00:56.124 --> 00:01:00.062
to guide a&nbsp;generative AI model towards a desired outcome.

00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:04.182
The&nbsp;simplest form of a RAG prompt structure
is shown&nbsp;on the right.

00:01:04.182 --> 00:01:13.082
Below, you'll see a user query, as well&nbsp;as relevant context related to that query. Answer&nbsp;the user query using the relevant context.

00:01:13.082 --> 00:01:21.158
Here,&nbsp;you'll provide the user query and the relevant&nbsp;context, while the generative AI model fills out&nbsp;your answer.

00:01:21.158 --> 00:01:27.903
While this may seem simple there are&nbsp;extra considerations that are worth mentioning&nbsp;when designing a RAG system.

00:01:27.903 --> 00:01:32.108
The first is that the&nbsp;basics of prompt
engineering apply strongly here.

00:01:32.108 --> 00:01:37.288
Language models perform best with clear,
structured&nbsp;and organized information.

00:01:37.288 --> 00:01:43.847
Some models will come&nbsp;with system prompts, where you provide separate&nbsp;instructions outlining the model's general&nbsp;behavior.

00:01:43.847 --> 00:01:50.780
Other models may require you to embed&nbsp;these instructions directly into the main context&nbsp;you provide them.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:56.806
The second one is confusing language can be problematic for models and people&nbsp;too,

00:01:56.806 --> 00:02:00.581
so it's crucial to communicate clearly and&nbsp;concisely.

00:02:00.581 --> 00:02:08.044
In particular, models perform better with&nbsp;
positive wording, i.e. instructing the model to do&nbsp;this,

00:02:08.044 --> 00:02:14.096
than with negative wording, i.e. instructing&nbsp;
the model to not do this.

00:02:14.096 --> 00:02:20.125
In addition to being&nbsp;clear and concise, providing a complete example of&nbsp;a desired output,

00:02:20.125 --> 00:02:25.220
also known as one-shot learning,
can improve model outputs.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:34.324
Many models including&nbsp;state-of-the-art models such as GPT4 and Claw 3&nbsp;Opus respond well to XML style formatting.

00:02:34.324 --> 00:02:39.930
XML is&nbsp;a well-organized structure with sections for where&nbsp;certain information goes

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.593
that is defined by its&nbsp;opening and
closing tags using angle braces.

00:02:44.593 --> 00:02:50.323
This&nbsp;makes it super clear to the model where the start&nbsp;and end of each relevant section is.

00:02:50.323 --> 00:02:55.739
On the right&nbsp;side of the slide, you'll see the previous RAG&nbsp;prompt reformatted to XML

00:02:55.739 --> 00:03:01.129
with the instructions, user query,
and relevant context sectioned out.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:05.143
Mention in the prompt that certain 
information&nbsp;may not be relevant.

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:13.283
If the language model thinks&nbsp;it must use all the information provided, it could&nbsp;lead to hallucinations when irrelevant details&nbsp;are included.

00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:21.680
Similarly, it helps to explicitly&nbsp;tell the model to admit when it doesn't have&nbsp;the information it needs to answer the question.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:30.000
Consider including metadata about the source&nbsp;documents to provide context, especially when&nbsp;using information from multiple sources.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.181
For&nbsp;example, you can include the following in the&nbsp;prompt to inform the language model what type of&nbsp;data it's using.

00:03:37.181 --> 00:03:41.585
Provided below is a user query&nbsp;and
some relevant information.

00:03:41.585 --> 00:03:46.212
You'll first see&nbsp;a summary of the source from which the relevant&nbsp;information comes from,

00:03:46.212 --> 00:03:48.889
then a direct passage from&nbsp;the source.

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:56.299
Use information from the direct passage&nbsp;to inform your answer, contextualizing it with the&nbsp;summary.

00:03:56.299 --> 00:04:00.802
However, using XML is not necessary to use&nbsp;these techniques.

00:04:00.802 --> 00:04:08.090
The vital point is that the&nbsp;text should be clearly structured. XML is just one&nbsp;popular example of such a structure.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:15.093
Another good&nbsp;reason to include chunk metadata in the prompt is&nbsp;that it allows the LLM to cite its sources.

00:04:15.093 --> 00:04:22.124
The new&nbsp;prompt will include the instructions to cite the&nbsp;source title and author, along with more contextual&nbsp;information

00:04:22.124 --> 00:04:25.106
that includes source title and source&nbsp;author.

00:04:25.106 --> 00:04:32.321
Provided below is a user query with some&nbsp;relevant information. You'll first see a&nbsp;direct passage from the source,

00:04:32.321 --> 00:04:35.376
then the source&nbsp;title and source author.

00:04:35.376 --> 00:04:40.044
Use information from&nbsp;chunk y's direct passage to 
inform your answer

00:04:40.044 --> 00:04:44.047
and then cite the source title and
author at&nbsp;the end of the answer.

00:04:44.047 --> 00:04:51.265
Finally, prompt engineering&nbsp;is a series of trial and error. Be ready to tweak&nbsp;and test the prompt over time.

00:04:51.265 --> 00:04:54.460
Language models&nbsp;can update and change behavior.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:59.737
Your source data&nbsp;may change or the requirements of your application&nbsp;may evolve over time.

00:04:59.737 --> 00:05:04.140
Whatever it is it is rarely a&nbsp;one-size-fits-all solution.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:12.193
It may be a good idea&nbsp;to invent a testing system whereby you can grade&nbsp;the quality of various prompts and the answers&nbsp;they offer.

00:05:12.193 --> 00:05:16.226
That concludes what you'll need to&nbsp;know about 
prompt engineering for RAG.

00:05:16.226 --> 00:05:24.201
Coming up&nbsp;next, you'll take what you learned and apply it&nbsp;by writing prompts that effectively use the vector&nbsp;databases search results

00:05:24.201 --> 00:05:26.120
to answer user questions.

